From: Sreesai Arjun <sreesaiarjun@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] AP_HAL_ChibiOS: book bdshot RECV_FAILED on the channel that failed

bdshot_receive_pulses_DMAR() rotates bdshot.curr_telem_chan to the next
input-capture channel before bdshot_send_pulse() books the outcome of the
previous receive.  The RECV_COMPLETE branch correctly uses prev_telem_chan,
but the RECV_FAILED branch (no edges captured: the ESC never answered)
used curr_telem_chan, i.e. the NEXT channel, and used it as a global
channel index although it is group-local.

Effect: the no-reply failures of a silent ESC inflate its neighbour's
erpm error rate, which AP_ESC_Telem logs (ESC message error_rate field)
and returns from get_raw_rpm_and_error_rate().  The silent ESC itself
never publishes a rate (update_rpm() is only called on a valid eRPM), so
the log points at the wrong motor.  Reproduced on the Nano H743
AIO (4 motors on TIM1) by firmware/bdshot_telem/test_bdshot_telem.cpp T8.

---
--- a/libraries/AP_HAL_ChibiOS/RCOutput_bdshot.cpp
+++ b/libraries/AP_HAL_ChibiOS/RCOutput_bdshot.cpp
@@ -284,7 +284,11 @@
             _bdshot.erpm_last_stats_ms[chan] = now;
         }
     } else if (group.dshot_state == DshotState::RECV_FAILED) {
-        _bdshot.erpm_errors[group.bdshot.curr_telem_chan]++;
+        // the DMA completion ISR has already rotated curr_telem_chan to the
+        // next channel, so the channel that failed to answer is
+        // prev_telem_chan; erpm_errors[] is indexed by global channel
+        // number, the same as the RECV_COMPLETE branch above.
+        _bdshot.erpm_errors[group.chan[group.bdshot.prev_telem_chan]]++;
     }
 
     if (group.bdshot.enabled) {
